D("refurbish",{etymology:E("From re- ‘again’ + furbish ‘polish up, renew’, through Old French forbir ‘polish, burnish’; refurbish first meant polish something up and later renovate it.","re-","forbir","re- + furbish","Etymonline, refurbish (https://www.etymonline.com/word/refurbish)."),contexts:[C("refurbish.s1","居家","The landlord refurbished the vacant flat with fresh paint, repaired cabinets, and safer wiring before renting it again.","房東重新粉刷空屋、修好櫥櫃並更新較安全的電線，整修後再出租。","To refurbish is to renovate or redecorate a room or building.","Refurbished takes the flat as its object; with lists the work done before renting.","The previously vacant flat is repaired and redecorated for use again.")],mistakes:[M("word-choice","The crew refurbished the damaged apartment by demolishing every wall and leaving the site as an empty lot.","The crew demolished the damaged apartment and left the site as an empty lot.","Refurbish means renovate or redecorate an existing room or building; total demolition removes the building instead.")],quiz:[Q("refurbish.q1","refurbish.s1","meaning","To refurbish an apartment is to ___.",["repair or redecorate it","turn it into a public vote","make it colder with refrigerant"],"repair or redecorate it","Refurbish means renovate or redecorate a room or building.","meaning"),Q("refurbish.q2","refurbish.s1","context","Which project refurbishes a library?",["Repairing its floor and repainting the reading room","Selling the land and removing the building","Listing the library in a bibliography"],"Repairing its floor and repainting the reading room","The existing building is renovated rather than removed.","context"),Q("refurbish.q3","refurbish.s1","collocation","The owners hired a contractor to ____ the old hotel before reopening.",["refurbish","refute","regain"],"refurbish","Refurbish a building means renovate it for renewed use.","collocation"),Q("refurbish.q4","refurbish.s1","word-formation","The prefix re- in refurbish contributes a sense of ___.",["renewing or doing again","negation","a person who acts"],"renewing or doing again","The word is based on furbish, ‘polish up’, with re- suggesting renewal.","word-formation")]});
D("refusal",{etymology:E("From refuse + -al, a noun-forming suffix; refuse came through Old French refuser from Vulgar Latin *refusare, related to Latin refundere ‘give back’.","re-","fundere","-al","Etymonline, refuse (https://www.etymonline.com/word/refuse)."),contexts:[C("refusal.s1","職場","Her refusal to sign the inaccurate report was recorded, along with her request to correct the figures first.","她拒絕簽署不準確的報告一事被記錄下來，她也要求先更正數字。","Refusal is the act of saying no to a request or demand.","Refusal is followed by to sign; the possessive her identifies who declined.","She explicitly says no to signing until the report is corrected.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The manager's refusal to approve the form meant that she had formally approved it.","The manager's refusal to approve the form meant that she had said no to it.","A refusal is a rejection, not an approval.")],quiz:[Q("refusal.q1","refusal.s1","meaning","A refusal to answer is ___.",["a decision to say no to answering","a promise to answer immediately","a published reference list"],"a decision to say no to answering","Refusal names the act of declining a request.","meaning"),Q("refusal.q2","refusal.s1","context","Which event is a refusal?",["The applicant says she will not sign the incomplete form.","The applicant signs the form and submits it.","The applicant reads the form aloud."],"The applicant says she will not sign the incomplete form.","She declines the requested action.","context"),Q("refusal.q3","refusal.s1","word-formation","Refusal is the noun related to the verb ___.",["refuse","refurbish","regenerate"],"refuse","The suffix -al forms the noun naming the act of refusing.","word-formation"),Q("refusal.q4","refusal.s1","collocation","The company sent a written ____ of the proposed terms.",["refusal","region","refraction"],"refusal","Written refusal is a common phrase for a formal rejection.","collocation")]});
D("refuse",{etymology:E("The verb came through Old French refuser from Vulgar Latin *refusare, from Latin refundere ‘give back’; the separate waste noun came from Old French refus, a rejected thing.","re-","fundere","verb/noun have related but distinct formations","Etymonline, refuse (https://www.etymonline.com/word/refuse)."),contexts:[C("refuse.s1","職場","He refused the promotion because it required moving his family before the school year ended.","他拒絕升遷，因為那需要在學年結束前搬家。","To refuse is to say that you will not do, accept, or give something.","Refused takes the promotion as its object; because explains the reason.","He decides not to accept the offered position."),C("refuse.s2","服務","The clerk refused the customer a refund because the return period had expired.","退貨期限已過，因此店員不准顧客退款。","To refuse someone something is to not allow them to have it.","Refused takes the person and requested item as objects; because gives the reason.","The clerk denies the customer's request for a refund."),C("refuse.s3","環境","The factory sorted its refuse into separate bins for paper, metal, and hazardous waste.","工廠將廢棄物分類，分別放入紙類、金屬與有害廢料桶。","Refuse as a noun means rubbish or waste, especially from homes or industry.","Refuse is the object of sorted; into introduces the categories.","The industrial waste is separated by material type.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The city collected household refuse, meaning residents' formal refusals to answer a request.","The city collected household refuse, meaning residents' rubbish and discarded material.","Refuse pronounced as a noun means rubbish; a refusal is the act of saying no and is a different form.")],quiz:[Q("refuse.q1","refuse.s1","meaning","If you refuse an invitation, you ___.",["say you will not accept it","send it to a reference book","cool it in a refrigerator"],"say you will not accept it","The verb means decline to do, accept, or give something.","meaning"),Q("refuse.q2","refuse.s2","context","The store refused Mina a refund. The store ___.",["did not allow Mina to receive her money back","paid Mina twice","gave Mina a recommendation"],"did not allow Mina to receive her money back","This construction means not allowing someone to have something.","context"),Q("refuse.q3","refuse.s3","meaning","In “industrial refuse was sorted,” refuse means ___.",["rubbish or waste","a public vote","a request that was accepted"],"rubbish or waste","The noun sense is waste material.","meaning"),Q("refuse.q4","refuse.s1","grammar","Choose the correct sentence.",["They refused to cross the unsafe bridge.","They refused crossing to the unsafe bridge.","They refused to crossing the unsafe bridge."],"They refused to cross the unsafe bridge.","Refuse is followed by a to-infinitive when declining an action.","grammar")]});
D("refute",{etymology:E("From Latin refutare ‘check, rebut, disprove’, possibly re- ‘back’ + a base connected with fūtis ‘prop’; English adopted it through French, with the core sense of defeating an argument.","re-","refutare",null,"Etymonline, refute (https://www.etymonline.com/word/refute)."),contexts:[C("refute.s1","科學","The new measurements refuted the claim that the bridge moved two meters during the night.","新的測量結果駁斥了橋樑夜間移動兩公尺的說法。","To refute an idea or statement is to prove that it is false.","Refuted takes the claim as its object; that-clause specifies what was disproved.","Measurements provide evidence against the stated claim."),C("refute.s2","公共討論","The spokesperson refuted the allegation in a firm statement but did not present evidence that settled the question.","發言人以堅定聲明否認該指控，但沒有提出能定論的證據。","In a looser use, refute can mean say firmly that something is not true.","Refuted takes allegation as its object; but contrasts the denial with lack of proof.","The spokesperson firmly denies the allegation without demonstrating its falsity.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The reporter refuted the allegation by merely denying it, then claimed that the unsupported denial proved the allegation false.","The reporter denied the allegation; to refute it, she would need evidence showing it was false.","Refute in its strict sense requires disproof by evidence; a bare denial only says the claim is untrue.")],quiz:[Q("refute.q1","refute.s1","meaning","To refute an argument is to ___.",["show with evidence that it is false","repeat it without comment","approve it in a public vote"],"show with evidence that it is false","The core sense is proving an idea or statement false.","meaning"),Q("refute.q2","refute.s2","context","A witness firmly says “That account is not true,” without offering proof. This is the looser sense of ___.",["refute","regenerate","refrigerate"],"refute","The entry includes the informal use ‘say firmly that something is not true.’","context"),Q("refute.q3","refute.s1","collocation","The scientist used repeated trials to ____ the claim.",["refute","refuel","regard"],"refute","Trials can provide evidence disproving a claim.","collocation"),Q("refute.q4","refute.s1","confusable","Which statement is correct?",["Evidence can refute a claim; a bare denial only rejects it.","Any disagreement automatically refutes a claim.","Refute means to give money back."],"Evidence can refute a claim; a bare denial only rejects it.","The stricter source sense requires proof of falsity.","confusable")]});
D("regain",{etymology:E("Formed from re- ‘back, again’ + gain, from Old French gaaignier ‘earn, acquire’; regain means obtain again something previously possessed.","re-","gaaignier","re- + gain","Etymonline, regain (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regain)."),contexts:[C("regain.s1","健康","With gradual exercise, the injured runner regained the strength she had lost during six weeks of bed rest.","透過循序漸進的運動，受傷的跑者恢復了臥床六週期間失去的力量。","To regain is to get back something you had before.","Regained takes strength as its object; that-clause identifies the lost capacity.","Exercise restores a level of strength she previously had.")],mistakes:[M("word-formation","The runner gained her strength for the first time after the injury, so she regained something she had never possessed.","The runner regained her former strength after the injury.","Regain means get back something previously held; a first-time acquisition is simply gain.")],quiz:[Q("regain.q1","regain.s1","meaning","To regain a lost key is to ___.",["get the key back","make a new key for the first time","refuse to accept a key"],"get the key back","The prefix re- signals getting something back again.","meaning"),Q("regain.q2","regain.s1","context","After months of therapy, the patient regained movement in her hand. She ___.",["recovered a function she had lost","learned to read a new book","made a referendum"],"recovered a function she had lost","Regain describes getting back a prior ability.","context"),Q("regain.q3","regain.s1","word-formation","Which contrast is right?",["Gain means acquire; regain means acquire back.","Regain means lose forever.","Gain and regain both mean refuse."],"Gain means acquire; regain means acquire back.","The prefix re- adds the idea of recovery or return.","word-formation"),Q("regain.q4","regain.s1","word-choice","The team hopes to ____ its place at the top of the league after last season's loss.",["regain","refute","refurbish"],"regain","The team wants to get back a position it previously held.","word-choice")]});
D("regal",{etymology:E("From Old French regal and Latin regalis ‘royal, kingly’, from rex (genitive regis) ‘king’; the remote root *reg- carried senses of directing or ruling.",null,"rex / regis","-al","Etymonline, regal (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regal)."),contexts:[C("regal.s1","建築","The theater's regal entrance had marble columns, a broad staircase, and a gold-painted ceiling.","劇院氣派堂皇的入口有大理石柱、寬闊階梯與金色天花板。","Regal describes something with the appearance of a king or queen, or something very grand.","Regal modifies entrance; the list of architectural details supports the impression.","The scale and decoration create a royal, imposing appearance.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The tiny plastic booth with a broken door looked regal, plain, cramped, and entirely lacking grandeur.","The tiny plastic booth looked shabby, plain, and entirely lacking grandeur.","Regal implies royal appearance or grandness, which conflicts with the explicitly plain, cramped booth.")],quiz:[Q("regal.q1","regal.s1","meaning","A regal hall looks ___.",["grand and kingly","plain and cramped","like a cooling fluid"],"grand and kingly","Regal means resembling a monarch or very grand.","meaning"),Q("regal.q2","regal.s1","context","Which detail makes a room seem regal?",["A high throne-like chair beneath a decorated gold canopy","A broken folding stool in a bare shed","A ballot box in a voting booth"],"A high throne-like chair beneath a decorated gold canopy","The furnishings give the room a royal, grand appearance.","context"),Q("regal.q3","regal.s1","word-choice","The palace's ____ staircase impressed every visitor.",["regal","refundable","refrigerant"],"regal","The staircase has an impressive, royal grandeur.","word-choice"),Q("regal.q4","regal.s1","meaning","If a coat has a regal appearance, it looks ___.",["fit for a king or queen","unprocessed and raw","easy to refund"],"fit for a king or queen","Regal means kingly or very grand.","meaning")]});
D("regard",{etymology:E("From Old French regarder ‘look at, take notice’, re- (perhaps intensive) + garder ‘look, heed’ from a Germanic source; the noun extended from looking to consideration, esteem, and greetings.","re-","garder","re- + garder","Etymonline, regard (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regard)."),contexts:[C("regard.s1","判斷","Most reviewers regard the revised schedule as fair because it gives every department the same notice period.","多數審查者認為修訂後的時程公平，因為每個部門都有相同通知期。","To regard someone or something as a stated way is to think of or consider it that way.","Regard takes schedule as its object and as fair as its complement; because gives the reason.","The reviewers judge the schedule to be fair."),C("regard.s2","觀察","The archivist regarded the damaged seal closely before deciding whether it was authentic.","檔案管理員仔細注視受損印章，才判斷它是否真品。","To regard can mean look at someone or something steadily.","Regarded takes seal as its object; closely modifies the act of looking.","The archivist examines the physical seal with sustained attention."),C("regard.s3","人際","The students regard their mentor with respect because she gives honest feedback and shares credit.","學生尊敬導師，因為她提供坦誠回饋並願意分享功勞。","Regard as a noun is admiration and care for someone or something.","With respect completes the verb regard; because explains why the admiration exists.","Their regard is esteem based on the mentor's conduct."),C("regard.s4","書信","Please give my regards to your family when you speak with them this weekend.","本週末和家人通話時，請代我向他們問好。","Regards in the plural means good wishes sent to someone.","Give my regards is a fixed expression; to your family identifies the recipients.","The speaker sends friendly greetings through the listener.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The committee regarded the proposal fairly because its members made a judgment, but wrote “regarded the proposal as fairly.”","The committee regarded the proposal as fair because its members judged it to be fair.","When regard means consider, the pattern is regard something as + adjective; fairly is an adverb and does not fit the complement slot.")],quiz:[Q("regard.q1","regard.s1","grammar","Complete: “They regard the rule ___ unnecessary.”",["as","with","from"],"as","Regard something as a quality is the standard complement pattern.","grammar"),Q("regard.q2","regard.s2","context","Which sentence uses regard to mean look steadily at?",["The child regarded the painting for several minutes.","The board regarded the plan as costly.","Send my regards to Alex."],"The child regarded the painting for several minutes.","The object of regard is visually observed for an extended time.","context"),Q("regard.q3","regard.s3","meaning","“She is held in high regard” means people ___.",["admire and respect her","look at her only once","send her a refund"],"admire and respect her","This is the esteem or admiration sense.","meaning"),Q("regard.q4","regard.s4","collocation","Before signing off, the letter says “Please give my ____ to your parents.”",["regards","regal","regiments"],"regards","Give my regards is a conventional way to send greetings.","collocation")]});
D("regarding",{etymology:E("The present participle of regard, from Old French regarder ‘look at, take notice’; as a preposition it developed the meaning ‘concerning’ or ‘as for’ a topic.","re-","garder","-ing","Etymonline, regard (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regard)."),contexts:[C("regarding.s1","職場","We received three questions regarding the revised travel policy and answered each one before the meeting.","我們收到三個關於修訂差旅政策的問題，並在會議前逐一回答。","Regarding means on the subject of something.","Regarding introduces the noun phrase the revised travel policy; and links receiving to answering.","The questions concern the new policy."),C("regarding.s2","信件","Regarding your request for a later appointment, the clinic can offer a time on Thursday afternoon.","至於您要求延後預約一事，診所可提供週四下午的時段。","Regarding can introduce the particular topic a speaker wants to discuss.","Regarding your request is a fronted topic phrase; the main clause provides the response.","The sentence marks the request as the topic before giving information about it.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","Regarding the revised timetable, were approved by the director yesterday.","The revised timetable was approved by the director yesterday.","Regarding introduces a topic phrase, but it cannot replace the sentence's subject; the original has no grammatical subject for were approved.")],quiz:[Q("regarding.q1","regarding.s1","meaning","“Questions regarding the invoice” are questions ___.",["about the invoice","that reject the invoice","that cool the invoice"],"about the invoice","Regarding means on the subject of.","meaning"),Q("regarding.q2","regarding.s2","context","Which opening uses regarding to introduce a topic?",["Regarding your application, the panel will reply next week.","The panel regarding your application next week reply.","Your application is regarding the panel."],"Regarding your application, the panel will reply next week.","The opening phrase marks the application as the topic.","context"),Q("regarding.q3","regarding.s1","collocation","The email includes an update ____ the delivery date.",["regarding","regal","regenerate"],"regarding","Update regarding a matter means update about that matter.","collocation"),Q("regarding.q4","regarding.s2","grammar","Choose the complete sentence after the topic phrase.",["Regarding the fee, we will send a separate notice.","Regarding the fee, was increased yesterday.","Regarding to the fee, we will send a notice."],"Regarding the fee, we will send a separate notice.","A topic phrase needs a complete clause after it; regarding takes a noun phrase directly.","grammar")]});
D("regardless",{etymology:E("From regard + -less, with -less meaning ‘without’; the adverb developed ‘without considering’ and commonly appears as regardless of.",null,"regard","-less","Etymonline, regard (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regard)."),contexts:[C("regardless.s1","日常生活","The train was delayed, but we reached the museum regardless and still saw the final exhibit.","火車雖然誤點，我們仍然抵達博物館，也看到了最後一件展品。","Regardless can mean despite what has been said or done; in this sentence it means anyway.","Regardless stands at the end of the clause; but contrasts the delay with their arrival.","The delay did not prevent them from going to the museum."),C("regardless.s2","安全","The hikers continued regardless of the warning signs and entered the closed canyon.","健行者不顧警告標誌繼續前進，進入已封閉的峽谷。","Regardless of means paying no attention to a difficulty or warning.","Regardless of is followed by the noun phrase warning signs; and links ignoring the signs with entry.","The hikers disregard the posted warning and proceed anyway.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","Regardless the warning signs, the hikers entered the closed canyon.","Regardless of the warning signs, the hikers entered the closed canyon.","When followed by a noun phrase, regardless requires the preposition of.")],quiz:[Q("regardless.q1","regardless.s1","meaning","“The team finished regardless” means it finished ___.",["anyway, despite the difficulty","only if nothing went wrong","after asking for a refund"],"anyway, despite the difficulty","Regardless can mean despite what happened.","meaning"),Q("regardless.q2","regardless.s2","grammar","Choose the correct phrase.",["regardless of the cost","regardless the cost","regardless to the cost"],"regardless of the cost","This use is followed by of before a noun phrase.","grammar"),Q("regardless.q3","regardless.s2","context","A driver goes regardless of the road closure. The driver ___.",["pays no attention to the closure","obeys the closure and stays home","asks for a regional vote"],"pays no attention to the closure","Regardless of means without paying attention to the obstacle.","context"),Q("regardless.q4","regardless.s1","word-choice","It began to rain, but the outdoor concert went on ____. ",["regardless","regal","regarding"],"regardless","The concert continued anyway despite the rain.","word-choice")]});
D("regenerate",{etymology:E("From Latin regenerare ‘bring forth again’, re- ‘again’ + generare ‘produce, beget’, from genus ‘kind’; English uses it for regrowth, renewal, and the state newly formed.","re-","generare","re- + generate","Etymonline, regenerate (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regenerate)."),contexts:[C("regenerate.s1","生物學","Some salamanders can regenerate a lost tail over time, growing new tissue where the injury healed.","有些蠑螈能隨時間使失去的尾巴再生，在傷口癒合處長出新組織。","To regenerate intransitively is to grow again after being damaged or lost.","Can regenerate is followed by a lost tail as the body part; over time marks duration.","The salamander grows tissue again after losing part of its body."),C("regenerate.s2","城市規劃","The grant helped regenerate the neglected waterfront by funding housing repairs, public paths, and small businesses.","這筆補助透過資助住宅修繕、公共步道與小型商家，幫助荒廢的海濱地區復興。","To regenerate an area or organization is to bring new energy and growth to it.","Helped regenerate takes waterfront as its object; by explains the revitalization measures.","The investments add new activity and improve the neglected district."),C("regenerate.s3","醫學","Researchers studied regenerate tissue that had formed again after a controlled wound in the laboratory model.","研究人員研究了實驗室模型在受控傷口後重新形成的新生組織。","Regenerate can be an adjective meaning newly grown or formed again.","Regenerate modifies tissue; that-clause identifies how it formed.","The tissue is newly formed after damage.")],mistakes:[M("word-choice","The salamander regenerated its missing tail by leaving the wound unchanged and growing no new tissue.","The salamander failed to regenerate its missing tail because no new tissue grew.","Regenerate means grow again; an unchanged wound with no growth shows regeneration did not occur.")],quiz:[Q("regenerate.q1","regenerate.s1","meaning","A lizard regenerates part of its tail when it ___.",["grows that part again after loss","refuses to move","reflects an image"],"grows that part again after loss","The intransitive sense is regrowth after damage or loss.","meaning"),Q("regenerate.q2","regenerate.s2","context","A city project regenerates a neglected district by ___.",["bringing new services, homes, and activity to it","closing every business and removing all residents","holding a sports match there"],"bringing new services, homes, and activity to it","This is revitalization of an area.","context"),Q("regenerate.q3","regenerate.s3","word-form","In “regenerate tissue,” regenerate is a/an ___.",["adjective meaning newly formed again","noun naming a public vote","adverb meaning regardless"],"adjective meaning newly formed again","The source includes an adjectival sense for newly grown or formed.","word form"),Q("regenerate.q4","regenerate.s2","collocation","The council plans to ____ the abandoned industrial waterfront.",["regenerate","refute","regard"],"regenerate","The council aims to bring new life and growth to the area.","collocation")]});
D("regeneration",{etymology:E("From Latin regenerationem, from regenerare ‘make over, generate again’, re- ‘again’ + generare ‘bring forth’; the noun names regrowth or the renewal of a place.","re-","generare","-ation","Etymonline, regenerate (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regenerate)."),contexts:[C("regeneration.s1","生物學","The doctor explained that skin regeneration would gradually replace the tissue damaged by the shallow burn.","醫師說明，皮膚再生會逐漸取代淺層燒傷造成的受損組織。","Regeneration is the natural regrowth of skin, forest, or energy.","Skin regeneration is the subject; would replace states the process and result.","New skin grows in place of damaged tissue."),C("regeneration.s2","城市規劃","The waterfront regeneration brought apartments, a public market, and a walking path to the neglected harbor district.","海濱重建為荒廢港區帶來公寓、公共市場與步道。","Regeneration can mean bringing new life to a poor or neglected area.","Waterfront regeneration is the subject; brought takes the new facilities as its object.","The investment gives the neglected district renewed activity.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The forest's regeneration meant that every burned tree remained dead and no new plants grew.","The forest's destruction meant that burned trees remained dead and no new plants grew.","Regeneration is regrowth or renewal; the stated absence of new growth describes destruction instead.")],quiz:[Q("regeneration.q1","regeneration.s1","meaning","Skin regeneration is ___.",["the natural regrowth of skin","the act of holding an election","the cooling of a machine"],"the natural regrowth of skin","This source sense describes regrowth after tissue damage.","meaning"),Q("regeneration.q2","regeneration.s2","context","A poor district's regeneration might include ___.",["new homes, services, and public spaces","closing the area permanently","making a mirror reflect light"],"new homes, services, and public spaces","These changes bring new life to the area.","context"),Q("regeneration.q3","regeneration.s1","word-formation","Regeneration is related to regenerate and means producing or growing ___.",["again","for the first time only","without change"],"again","The prefix re- means again in this word family.","word-formation"),Q("regeneration.q4","regeneration.s2","collocation","The council approved a program of urban ____ for the old harbor.",["regeneration","refrigerant","refusal"],"regeneration","Urban regeneration describes revitalizing a neglected area.","collocation")]});
D("regent",{etymology:E("From Latin regentem, present participle of regere ‘rule, direct’; a regent rules temporarily for a sovereign who is unable to govern.",null,"regere","-ent","Etymonline, regent (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regent)."),contexts:[C("regent.s1","歷史","The queen appointed a regent to govern until her young heir came of age and could rule independently.","女王任命攝政者代為治理，直到年幼的繼承人成年並能獨立統治。","A regent governs in place of a sovereign who cannot rule yet.","Appointed a regent is followed by an infinitive stating the role; until marks its end condition.","The regent temporarily exercises the monarch's governing authority.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The regent inherited the throne and ruled as sovereign for life after the rightful monarch became able to govern.","The regent governed temporarily in place of the monarch until the monarch could rule.","A regent acts on behalf of a sovereign who cannot rule, rather than replacing an available monarch for life.")],quiz:[Q("regent.q1","regent.s1","meaning","A regent rules ___.",["temporarily on behalf of a sovereign unable to govern","as a referee in a match","as a voter in a referendum"],"temporarily on behalf of a sovereign unable to govern","The regent governs in the sovereign's place until able to rule.","meaning"),Q("regent.q2","regent.s1","context","A child monarch is too young to govern. Who may act as regent?",["A guardian appointed to govern until the monarch comes of age","A new monarch elected for life","A librarian who lists references"],"A guardian appointed to govern until the monarch comes of age","The guardian rules temporarily for the young sovereign.","context"),Q("regent.q3","regent.s1","word-choice","The king was ill, so the council named a ____ to govern in his place.",["regent","refinery","refresher"],"regent","A regent governs when the sovereign cannot.","word-choice"),Q("regent.q4","regent.s1","confusable","A regent differs from a sovereign because the regent ___.",["governs in the sovereign's place, usually temporarily","always owns a refinery","is a written source citation"],"governs in the sovereign's place, usually temporarily","The source defines the role as acting for an unable sovereign.","confusable")]});
D("regime",{etymology:E("From French régime and Latin regimen ‘rule, direction’, from regere ‘guide, rule’; English uses include a government, an organizing system, and a fixed health routine.",null,"regere","-e","Etymonline, regimen (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regimen)."),contexts:[C("regime.s1","政治","The opposition criticized the regime for banning independent newspapers and imprisoning peaceful demonstrators.","反對派批評該政權禁止獨立報紙並監禁和平示威者。","A regime is a government, often one criticized as strict or undemocratic.","Regime is the object of criticized; for introduces the grounds of criticism.","The government is described as authoritarian and restrictive."),C("regime.s2","管理","The laboratory introduced a strict data-protection regime that limits access and records every file transfer.","實驗室建立嚴格的資料保護制度，限制存取並記錄每次檔案傳輸。","A regime can be a method or system for organizing something.","Regime is modified by data-protection; that-clause describes the system's rules.","The term names an organized set of controls, not a government."),C("regime.s3","健康","Her doctor recommended a daily exercise regime with gentle stretches and two short walks.","醫師建議她採用每日運動規律，包括輕柔伸展與兩次短程步行。","A regime can mean a fixed plan of food, exercise, or medical treatment.","Regime is followed by with, which lists parts of the plan.","The schedule organizes regular exercise for health.")],mistakes:[M("grammar","The regime were criticized for banning independent newspapers and imprisoning peaceful demonstrators.","The regime was criticized for banning independent newspapers and imprisoning peaceful demonstrators.","Regime is a singular count noun, so it takes the singular verb was.")],quiz:[Q("regime.q1","regime.s1","meaning","A country's regime is its ___.",["government or system of rule","food and exercise plan only","list of sources in a book"],"government or system of rule","This is the political sense of regime.","meaning"),Q("regime.q2","regime.s2","context","A cybersecurity regime sets rules for passwords, access, and data storage. It is ___.",["an organizing system","a monarch's temporary deputy","a piece of waste"],"an organizing system","Regime can mean a system for organizing a domain.","context"),Q("regime.q3","regime.s3","meaning","An exercise regime is ___.",["a fixed plan for regular exercise","a government that bans newspapers","a mirror image"],"a fixed plan for regular exercise","The health sense is a planned routine of exercise or treatment.","meaning"),Q("regime.q4","regime.s1","register","Why may writers use regime carefully for a government?",["It often suggests strict or undemocratic rule.","It always means a harmless exercise plan.","It is the name of a light-measuring unit."],"It often suggests strict or undemocratic rule.","The source notes that the political sense is often critical.","register")]});
D("regimen",{etymology:E("From Old French regimen and Latin regimen ‘rule, guidance, government’, from regere ‘direct, guide’; it first described medical rules for diet and exercise.",null,"regere","-en","Etymonline, regimen (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regimen)."),contexts:[C("regimen.s1","醫療","The dermatologist prescribed a simple skin-care regimen: gentle cleanser each evening and sunscreen every morning.","皮膚科醫師開立簡單的護膚療程：每晚使用溫和潔面乳，每早塗防曬。","A regimen is a fixed plan for food, exercise, treatment, or daily life.","Regimen is followed by a colon that lists its scheduled steps.","The instructions form a regular routine of treatment.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The doctor prescribed a regimen that changed at random every hour and had no fixed steps.","The doctor prescribed a flexible treatment plan, not a fixed regimen.","A regimen is a fixed plan or routine, so a schedule changing randomly each hour does not fit.")],quiz:[Q("regimen.q1","regimen.s1","meaning","A medical regimen is ___.",["a fixed plan for treatment or daily health practices","a political government","a public election"],"a fixed plan for treatment or daily health practices","Regimen describes an organized health routine.","meaning"),Q("regimen.q2","regimen.s1","context","Which is a training regimen?",["A planned schedule of runs, rest days, and strength exercises","A one-time sprint with no plan","A list of references at the end of a report"],"A planned schedule of runs, rest days, and strength exercises","The fixed training routine is a regimen.","context"),Q("regimen.q3","regimen.s1","confusable","Which spelling usually names a health routine?",["regimen","regiment","regent"],"regimen","Regimen is the fixed plan; regiment is usually a military unit or a verb meaning control rigidly.","confusable"),Q("regimen.q4","regimen.s1","collocation","The physician adjusted her medication ____ after reviewing side effects.",["regimen","referee","regal"],"regimen","Medication regimen is a natural phrase for a planned course of treatment.","collocation")]});
D("regiment",{etymology:E("From Old French regiment and Late Latin regimentum ‘rule, direction’, from regere ‘rule, guide’; the military unit sense developed from organized command, and the verb means impose rigid organization.",null,"regere","-ment","Etymonline, regiment (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regiment)."),contexts:[C("regiment.s1","軍事","The colonel ordered the regiment to move before dawn, while support units secured the bridge.","上校命令該團在黎明前行動，支援部隊則負責確保橋梁安全。","A regiment is a large military unit, usually commanded by a colonel.","Ordered the regiment is followed by an infinitive giving the command; while contrasts support units.","The regiment is a sizable organized group of soldiers."),C("regiment.s2","修辭","A regiment of volunteers lined the riverbank to pass sandbags to the flood-response crews.","一大群志工沿河岸排開，將沙包傳給防洪隊伍。","Regiment can informally mean a large number or group of people or things.","A regiment of volunteers is a large-group phrase; to pass explains their purpose.","The phrase emphasizes the large number of volunteers, not a formal army unit."),C("regiment.s3","教育","The new principal refused to regiment every classroom into an identical script, allowing teachers to adapt lessons.","新校長拒絕把每間教室都嚴格編成完全相同的教學腳本，讓教師能調整課程。","To regiment is to organize and control people in a rigid way.","Refused to regiment takes classrooms as its object; allowing states the consequence of the policy.","The principal resists imposing uniform routines on all teachers.")],mistakes:[M("confusable","The doctor called a colonel-led military unit a health regiment because it prescribed the soldiers' daily meals.","The doctor called the soldiers' fixed health plan a regimen; their military unit was a regiment.","Regiment usually names a military unit, while regimen names a fixed health plan; the examples are distinct.")],quiz:[Q("regiment.q1","regiment.s1","meaning","A regiment in the army is ___.",["a large organized unit of soldiers","a fixed diet plan","a text citation"],"a large organized unit of soldiers","This is the military noun sense.","meaning"),Q("regiment.q2","regiment.s2","context","“A regiment of reporters arrived” means ___.",["a large number of reporters arrived","one colonel arrived alone","the reporters followed a diet plan"],"a large number of reporters arrived","The informal sense uses regiment for a large group.","context"),Q("regiment.q3","regiment.s3","meaning","To regiment workers is to ___.",["organize and control them rigidly","give them a refund","let them choose every step freely"],"organize and control them rigidly","The verb means impose a strict system of control.","meaning"),Q("regiment.q4","regiment.s1","confusable","A regiment is different from a regimen because a regiment is ___.",["a military unit; a regimen is a fixed routine","a treatment plan; a regiment is a word for a king","a public vote; a regimen is a reference list"],"a military unit; a regimen is a fixed routine","The similar forms have distinct meanings and use.","confusable")]});
D("regimentation",{etymology:E("From regiment + -ation, a noun of action; regiment derives from Latin regere ‘rule, guide’, and the noun describes rigid control that imposes uniform routines.",null,"regere","-ation","Etymonline, regiment (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regiment)."),contexts:[C("regimentation.s1","教育","The students complained about regimentation because every class followed the same minute-by-minute script with no room for discussion.","學生抱怨過度嚴格的編管，因為每堂課都逐分鐘照同一套腳本進行，沒有討論空間。","Regimentation is control that forces people into uniform routines.","Complained about regimentation is followed by because-clause explaining the uniformity.","The rigid script removes individual choice and variation.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The school's regimentation gave each teacher freedom to choose a different schedule and adapt every lesson independently.","The school's flexibility gave each teacher freedom to choose a different schedule and adapt every lesson independently.","Regimentation means strict uniform control, which contradicts the freedom described.")],quiz:[Q("regimentation.q1","regimentation.s1","meaning","Regimentation is ___.",["strict control that makes people follow uniform routines","a plan for returning money","the act of reading a song chorus"],"strict control that makes people follow uniform routines","The source sense emphasizes forced uniformity.","meaning"),Q("regimentation.q2","regimentation.s1","context","Which workplace shows regimentation?",["Workers must follow identical timed steps and cannot adjust them.","Workers set flexible hours and choose their methods.","Workers vote on a single political question."],"Workers must follow identical timed steps and cannot adjust them.","Rigid uniform routines characterize regimentation.","context"),Q("regimentation.q3","regimentation.s1","word-formation","Regimentation is the noun associated with the verb ___.",["regiment","regain","regard"],"regiment","The noun names the act or system of regimenting people.","word-formation"),Q("regimentation.q4","regimentation.s1","word-choice","The novel criticizes the factory's ____ of workers into identical schedules.",["regimentation","regeneration","regalness"],"regimentation","The factory imposes uniform routines on workers.","word-choice")]});
D("regimented",{etymology:E("Past-participle adjective from regiment, whose verb means organize and control rigidly; regiment traces to Late Latin regimentum and regere ‘rule, direct’.",null,"regere","-ed","Etymonline, regiment (https://www.etymonline.com/word/regiment)."),contexts:[C("regimented.s1","工作","In the regimented workshop, each task began at a fixed minute and workers had to follow the same sequence.","在管理嚴格的工作坊裡，每項工作都在固定分鐘開始，員工必須遵循相同流程。","Regimented means controlled so tightly that every action follows a set pattern.","Regimented modifies workshop; each and same emphasize the imposed uniformity.","The fixed timing and mandatory sequence show tight control.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The regimented schedule changed freely each day, with no assigned order, rules, or fixed activities.","The flexible schedule changed freely each day, with no assigned order or fixed activities.","Regimented means tightly controlled and patterned, not free from routines.")],quiz:[Q("regimented.q1","regimented.s1","meaning","A regimented routine is ___.",["tightly controlled and follows a set pattern","random and without any rules","a book used for reference"],"tightly controlled and follows a set pattern","The adjective describes actions controlled into a fixed sequence.","meaning"),Q("regimented.q2","regimented.s1","context","Which day is regimented?",["Every activity starts at a set time and follows the same required order.","The person decides spontaneously what to do each hour.","The day is spent reading one optional novel."],"Every activity starts at a set time and follows the same required order.","The fixed schedule shows strict control.","context"),Q("regimented.q3","regimented.s1","word-form","In “a regimented school day,” regimented is a/an ___.",["adjective describing the schedule","noun meaning a single soldier","verb in the future tense"],"adjective describing the schedule","It modifies school day by describing its rigid organization.","word form"),Q("regimented.q4","regimented.s1","word-choice","The tightly ____ procedure left no room for staff to change the order.",["regimented","refundable","refreshed"],"regimented","The procedure forces a fixed sequence.","word-choice")]});
